## A Year That's Anything But Fresh

With over 500 recalls this year alone, the food industry continues to struggle with contamination and safety issues. Staggeringly, 144 of these were classified as Class I recalls--the most severe type, indicating a reasonable probability that the use of or exposure to a violative product will cause serious adverse health consequences or death. These aren't numbers to ignore.

## Recent Culprits: Salmonella Dominates

From seasoned snacks to sweet treats, Salmonella contamination remains a major player in food recalls. Leading the charge, we've recently seen the likes of Ghirardelli Chocolate Company recalling their products due to this persistent pathogen. It's clear manufacturers must do more to prevent these risks from reaching consumers' shelves.

## Repeat Offenders

Brands like Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC stand out for their consistent lack of adherence to safety standards. For Moonlight Packing Corporation alone, all 11 of this year's recalls were Class I, which should set alarm bells ringing for shoppers.

## April Showers Bring... More Recalls?

May alone saw a record-breaking 173 recalls, making it the highest month so far this year. The reasons ranged from Salmonella (a frequent flier) to under-processed products risking Botulinum growth. It seems a spring cleaning of factory protocols is well overdue for some manufacturers.

## Staying Safe

In light of these findings, stay vigilant. Check recall announcements often. Wash fresh produce thoroughly. And perhaps think twice before reaching for products from manufacturers with a history of recalls, such as Wellnov Supplements LLC and Diva Fam Inc.
